

## Non‑negotiables (so joins + aggregations don’t break)

* Use the `/siren`-prefixed search endpoint (e.g. `POST /siren/<parent_index>/_search`), because the join query is provided by the plugin and is executed like a normal Elasticsearch query DSL search. [^1]  
* The `join` clause *filters the parent set* (the index you search on) using a child set (the `indices` inside the join), and you can **nest joins inside the `request.query`** to build multi-hop plans. [^1] [^3]  
* Join keys must be comparable (same datatype) and should be doc-values friendly (typically `keyword` fields like `id.keyword`, not analyzed `text`). [^3]  
* If you want aggregations driven by joined data, **project child fields** and then build **runtime fields** (via `runtime_mappings`) to compute metrics (sum/avg/count) from the projected multi-valued fields; this is the canonical way shown by Siren for aggregating projected fields. [Source](https://docs.siren.io/siren-federate-user-guide/39/joining-data.html) [^3]  
* You can pick a join algorithm via `type` (e.g. `BROADCAST_JOIN`, `HASH_JOIN`, `INDEX_JOIN`, `ROUTING_JOIN`) or let the planner choose. [^3]  

