## Aggregation “building blocks” you can reuse anywhere (the part that makes this general)

* **Count of matched child docs per parent** (after projecting any child field, `size()` is your count): `emit(doc.<project_alias>.size())`. [Source](https://docs.siren.io/siren-federate-user-guide/39/joining-data.html)  
* **Sum/avg/min/max over child numeric values per parent**: iterate `for (v in doc.<project_alias>)` and `emit(...)`, then aggregate those per-parent scalars with `sum/avg/stats` at aggregation time. [Source](https://docs.siren.io/siren-federate-user-guide/39/joining-data.html)  
* **Distribution aggregations over individual child values** (terms/histogram/percentiles on “raw child values”): create a runtime field that emits *each* projected value (`for (...) emit(v);`), then point `terms`, `histogram`, `percentiles`, etc. at that runtime field. [Source](https://docs.siren.io/siren-federate-user-guide/39/joining-data.html)  
* **Multi-hop join debugging / preserving join structure**: use `federate_object` runtime fields to project structured objects through nested joins (helps avoid Cartesian-product duplication), but note that sorting/joining on `federate_object` isn’t supported. [^3] [Source](https://docs.siren.io/siren-federate-user-guide/39/joining-data.html)  

